To use teeth that long, a cat needs to open its mouth very wide. Otherwise the lower jaw would be in the way. Smilodon could open its jaws to about 110 degrees. A lion manages about 65. Its skull had special joints and muscle attachments that made this possible.

In 2007, scientists built a detailed [[@computers-how|computer model]] of a Smilodon skull and tested it like an engineer tests a bridge. The skull was strong when pushing down with the neck, but weaker than a lion's when pulling sideways. That suggested a quick, controlled bite into a held-down animal, rather than a long struggle.

